        "---\n",
        "title: \"Autoregressive Language Models\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Next-token prediction, likelihood, and mathematical generation\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "This chapter begins the part of the book on large language models.  The goal is to explain an autoregressive language model as a clean mathematical object: a conditional probability model for the next token.  Once this viewpoint is clear, training, perplexity, temperature, top-$k$ sampling, nucleus sampling, greedy decoding, and beam search become consequences of probability and optimization rather than mysterious engineering tricks.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Write the probability of a token sequence using the autoregressive chain rule.\n",
        "- Explain next-token prediction as conditional distribution estimation.\n",
        "- Derive the negative log-likelihood and cross-entropy objective for language modeling.\n",
        "- Compute sentence log-probability and perplexity from next-token probabilities.\n",
        "- Compare greedy decoding, sampling, temperature scaling, top-$k$ sampling, nucleus sampling, and beam search.\n",
        "- Explain why causal masking is required in decoder-only Transformer language models.\n",
        "- Implement a small autoregressive generator and decoding methods from scratch in Python.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.1 The basic object: a distribution on sequences\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $V$ be a finite vocabulary with $|V|=d$.  A token sequence of length $T$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{1:T} = (w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_T) \\in V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A language model assigns probabilities to sequences.  For a fixed length $T$, this means a probability mass function\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T}), \\qquad w_{1:T} \\in V^T,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "satisfying\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T}) \\geq 0, \\qquad \\sum_{w_{1:T} \\in V^T} P(w_{1:T}) = 1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The space $V^T$ has size $d^T$, so directly specifying $P$ is impossible except for tiny vocabularies and tiny lengths.  The central mathematical idea is to factor the joint distribution into conditional distributions.\n",
        "\n",
        "By the chain rule of probability,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "P(w_1)P(w_2 \\mid w_1)P(w_3 \\mid w_{1:2})\\cdots P(w_T \\mid w_{1:T-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^{T} P(w_t \\mid w_{<t}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $w_{<t}=(w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1})$.  This identity is exact.  An autoregressive language model is a parameterized approximation of these conditional probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Autoregressive viewpoint\n",
        "\n",
        "An **autoregressive language model** estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_t \\mid w_{<t})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for each position $t$.  The model then defines a sequence distribution by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^{T} p_\\theta(w_t \\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The word *autoregressive* means that the model generates each new token using previously generated tokens as input.  In time series notation, the future is predicted from the past.  In NLP notation, the next token is predicted from the prefix.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.2 Prefixes, contexts, and conditional distributions\n",
        "\n",
        "For every prefix $c=w_{<t}$, an autoregressive model outputs a probability vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid c) \\in \\Delta^{d-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta^{d-1}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\left\\{p\\in R^d: p_j\\geq 0,\\; \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j=1\\right\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is the probability simplex.  If the vocabulary is ordered as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{v_1,\\ldots,v_d\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then the model output is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(\\cdot \\mid c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\left(p_\\theta(v_1\\mid c),\\ldots,p_\\theta(v_d\\mid c)\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A neural language model usually produces real-valued logits\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_\\theta(c) \\in R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and converts them to probabilities by the softmax map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(v_j\\mid c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp(z_j(c))}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{d}\\exp(z_\\ell(c))}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The logits are scores.  The softmax turns scores into a categorical distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important}\n",
        "## Representation, model, and probability\n",
        "\n",
        "For a prefix $c$, a modern neural language model usually has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c \\longmapsto h_\\theta(c) \\in R^m\n",
        "\\longmapsto z_\\theta(c)=Wh_\\theta(c)+b \\in R^d\n",
        "\\longmapsto p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid c)=\\operatorname{softmax}(z_\\theta(c)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The representation $h_\\theta(c)$ is not itself a probability distribution.  The probability distribution appears after the output logits are transformed by softmax.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.3 Training data and maximum likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the training corpus consists of token sequences\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D=\\{w^{(i)}_{1:T_i}: i=1,\\ldots,n\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The likelihood of the data under an autoregressive model is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\theta;D)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{i=1}^{n}\\prod_{t=1}^{T_i}\n",
        "p_\\theta\\left(w_t^{(i)} \\mid w_{<t}^{(i)}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\theta;D)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{T_i}\n",
        "\\log p_\\theta\\left(w_t^{(i)} \\mid w_{<t}^{(i)}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Training usually minimizes the negative log-likelihood,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal L(\\theta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{t=1}^{T_i}\n",
        "\\log p_\\theta\\left(w_t^{(i)} \\mid w_{<t}^{(i)}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is also the sum of cross-entropy losses.  At each position $t$, the target is the one-hot vector for the observed next token.  If $y_t$ is the index of $w_t$, then the one-position loss is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_t(\\theta)=-\\log p_\\theta(y_t\\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For logits $z\\in R^d$ and target class $y$, the gradient of the softmax cross-entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z_j}\\left[-\\log \\operatorname{softmax}(z)_y\\right]\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\operatorname{softmax}(z)_j - 1\\{j=y\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This simple formula is one reason next-token prediction scales well computationally.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.4 Teacher forcing\n",
        "\n",
        "During training, the model is given the true prefix from the corpus.  For example, from the sentence\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models predict the next token\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "we form training pairs:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "prefix: language                 target: models\n",
        "prefix: language models          target: predict\n",
        "prefix: language models predict  target: the\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This training approach is called **teacher forcing**.  The model learns from correct prefixes even though, at generation time, it must condition on tokens that it generated itself.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, training minimizes empirical conditional cross-entropy under the empirical distribution of true prefixes.  Generation samples from the model distribution over its own prefixes.  This mismatch is one source of error accumulation in generated text.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.5 Sentence probability, average loss, and perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "For one sequence $w_{1:T}$, the model log-probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log p_\\theta(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The average negative log-likelihood per token is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\bar{\\mathcal L}\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\frac{1}{T}\n",
        "\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PPL}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\exp(\\bar{\\mathcal L}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Perplexity can be interpreted as an effective average number of plausible next-token choices.  Lower perplexity usually means the model assigns higher probability to the observed text, but perplexity is not a complete measure of usefulness or truthfulness.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-warning}\n",
        "## Perplexity is not understanding\n",
        "\n",
        "A model can have low perplexity on a dataset by learning strong statistical regularities.  This does not imply that it reasons correctly, uses facts reliably, or understands meaning in a human sense.  Perplexity is a likelihood-based metric, not a complete evaluation of language behavior.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.6 Decoder-only Transformers as autoregressive models\n",
        "\n",
        "A decoder-only Transformer represents the prefix $w_{<t}$ using a stack of causal self-attention blocks.  If the input sequence is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{1:T},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then each position $t$ produces a hidden vector $h_t\\in R^m$.  The output logits for the next token are often computed by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "z_t = Wh_t + b \\in R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the next-token distribution is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_{t+1}=v_j\\mid w_{1:t})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp((z_t)_j)}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{d}\\exp((z_t)_\\ell)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The causal mask ensures that the hidden vector at position $t$ cannot use tokens from positions greater than $t$.  Without causal masking, a language model trained to predict the next token could leak information from the future.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a sequence length $T$, the causal attention mask has entries\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "M_{ij}=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "0, & j\\leq i,\\\\\n",
        "-\\infty, & j>i.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The attention weights are computed as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A=\\operatorname{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}+M\\right),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where the softmax is applied row by row.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.7 Generation as repeated sampling\n",
        "\n",
        "Once a model produces $p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid c)$, generation repeats three steps:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Compute logits $z_\\theta(c)$ for the current context $c$.\n",
        "2. Convert logits to a probability distribution, possibly modified by a decoding rule.\n",
        "3. Choose the next token and append it to the context.\n",
        "\n",
        "This gives a recursive stochastic process:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_t \\sim p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid W_{<t}),\n",
        "\\qquad t=1,2,\\ldots.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Different decoding rules define different stochastic or deterministic processes.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.8 Greedy decoding\n",
        "\n",
        "Greedy decoding chooses the most likely next token:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat w_t\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\max_{v\\in V} p_\\theta(v\\mid \\hat w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Greedy decoding is deterministic.  It is simple and often produces fluent text, but it can become repetitive and may miss globally better sequences.  It is a local optimization rule, not a global maximum-likelihood sequence search.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.9 Temperature scaling\n",
        "\n",
        "Given logits $z\\in R^d$, temperature scaling defines\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j(\\tau)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{\\exp(z_j/\\tau)}{\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{d}\\exp(z_\\ell/\\tau)},\n",
        "\\qquad \\tau>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Small temperature sharpens the distribution.  Large temperature flattens it.  As $\\tau\\to 0$, the distribution concentrates on the largest logit.  As $\\tau\\to \\infty$, it approaches the uniform distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "Temperature does not change the ordering of logits, but it changes entropy.  The entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "H(p(\\tau))=-\\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_j(\\tau)\\log p_j(\\tau).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In applications, temperature is a diversity-control parameter.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.10 Top-$k$ sampling\n",
        "\n",
        "Top-$k$ sampling keeps only the $k$ highest-probability tokens.  Let $S_k$ be the set of $k$ token indices with largest probabilities.  The top-$k$ distribution is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "q_j\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "\\dfrac{p_j}{\\sum_{\\ell\\in S_k}p_\\ell}, & j\\in S_k,\\\\\n",
        "0, & j\\notin S_k.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then the next token is sampled from $q$.  Top-$k$ sampling prevents extremely unlikely tokens from being selected while still allowing randomness among likely tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.11 Nucleus sampling, or top-$p$ sampling\n",
        "\n",
        "Nucleus sampling chooses the smallest set $S_p$ of high-probability tokens whose cumulative probability is at least a threshold $p_0$.  After sorting probabilities in descending order,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_{(1)}\\geq p_{(2)}\\geq \\cdots \\geq p_{(d)},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "choose the smallest $m$ such that\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{r=1}^{m}p_{(r)}\\geq p_0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The nucleus is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "S_p=\\{(1),\\ldots,(m)\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then probabilities are renormalized on $S_p$.  Unlike top-$k$, the number of allowed tokens changes with the confidence of the distribution.  If the distribution is sharp, the nucleus may be small.  If the distribution is flat, the nucleus may be larger.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.12 Beam search\n",
        "\n",
        "Beam search is an approximate search for a high-probability sequence.  It maintains a set of $B$ candidate prefixes called the beam.  At each step, each candidate prefix is extended by possible next tokens, and only the $B$ highest-scoring extended prefixes are kept.\n",
        "\n",
        "The score of a sequence is usually its log-probability:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_{<t}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Because log-probabilities are usually negative, longer sequences tend to have smaller scores.  Some implementations use length normalization, for example\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_\\alpha(w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{T^\\alpha}\n",
        "\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\log p_\\theta(w_t\\mid w_{<t}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\alpha\\geq 0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Beam search is common in translation and structured generation, but for open-ended generation it can produce dull or repetitive text because it aggressively seeks high-probability continuations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.13 Worked example: likelihood and perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a vocabulary contains four tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{\\texttt{math},\\texttt{models},\\texttt{learn},\\texttt{data}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A model assigns the following next-token probabilities to the observed sequence\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "math models learn data\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "at the four positions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0.40,\\quad 0.25,\\quad 0.50,\\quad 0.20.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The sequence probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0.40\\cdot 0.25\\cdot 0.50\\cdot 0.20=0.01.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-probability is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log(0.01)\\approx -4.6052.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The average negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-\\frac{1}{4}\\log(0.01)\\approx 1.1513.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\exp(1.1513)\\approx 3.1623.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This means that, on average, the model behaves as if it were choosing among about $3.16$ equally likely next-token options at each step.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.14 Python example: softmax, likelihood, and perplexity"
