        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Prompt 2: decoding audit\n",
        "\n",
        "Ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Explain the difference between temperature, top-k sampling, and nucleus sampling using probability notation.  Give one example where top-k and top-p keep different numbers of tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then verify the explanation by computing the filtered distributions yourself.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Prompt 3: perplexity interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "Ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> A language model has average negative log-likelihood 1.7 nats per token.  What is its perplexity?  What does this number mean, and what does it not mean?\n",
        "\n",
        "A good answer should mention $\\exp(1.7)$ and should not claim that perplexity measures factual correctness.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.22 Common mistakes\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Confusing the chain rule with a Markov assumption.**  The factorization $P(w_{1:T})=\\prod_t P(w_t\\mid w_{<t})$ is exact.  A Markov model makes the additional approximation $P(w_t\\mid w_{<t})\\approx P(w_t\\mid w_{t-r:t-1})$.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Treating logits as probabilities.**  Logits can be any real numbers.  Probabilities appear after softmax.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Ignoring length in sequence scores.**  The raw log-probability of a sequence usually decreases as length increases.  Comparing sequences of different lengths requires care.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **Believing sampling is the same as training.**  Training usually uses teacher forcing.  Generation conditions on previously generated tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. **Thinking low perplexity guarantees high-quality answers.**  Perplexity is a likelihood metric.  It does not by itself measure truthfulness, fairness, safety, or usefulness.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 17.23 Exercises\n",
        "\n",
